# SLATE mobile base udev rules -- install on the HOST, not in the container.
#
# Docker cannot create these: udev runs in the host kernel's device manager, and
# a container only ever sees the device nodes the host already made. This is the
# one part of the setup that is per-machine rather than baked into the image.
#
#   sudo cp 99-trossen-slate.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger && sudo udevadm settle
#
# Verify with the base plugged in and powered:  ls -l /dev/ttySLATE
#
# The base's USB-serial bridge is a CH340 (QinHeng Electronics), VID:PID
# 1a86:7523. That is a different chip from the U2D2's FTDI 0403:6014 in
# ../../middle-arm/host-setup/, so the two rules cannot collide and both may be
# installed on the same host.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE SYMLINK IS AND IS NOT FOR
#
# /dev/ttySLATE is for YOU, not for the driver. interbotix_slate_driver does not
# accept a port: base_driver::chassisInit() takes an output string, enumerates
# USB serial devices with libudev, matches idVendor/idProduct itself, and then
# reports which node it opened. There is no port parameter to point at this
# symlink and no equivalent of ../../middle-arm's DXL_PORT.
#
# It earns its place anyway, because "is the base actually connected?" is the
# first question every time the driver fails to start, and `ls -l /dev/ttySLATE`
# answers it without grepping lsusb. launch-base.sh checks for it before
# starting the node, so the failure is one clear line instead of a fatal deep in
# the driver.
#
# The corollary: with two CH340 devices attached, this symlink races to whichever
# enumerated last AND the driver picks whichever its scan hits first -- and those
# need not be the same device. Key on the adapter's serial if that ever happens,
# the same way ../../middle-arm/host-setup/99-interbotix-udev.rules documents for
# multiple U2D2s. Note that many CH340s ship with no serial descriptor at all, in
# which case the honest fix is keying on the physical USB port path (KERNELS==)
# rather than the device.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# MODE 0666 rather than GROUP="dialout": it matches what the Interbotix rules
# already do for ttyUSB on this host, and it means the container works whether or
# not the user inside it happens to be in dialout.
#
# ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE keeps ModemManager's hands off. Without it, ModemManager
# probes any new ttyUSB looking for a cellular modem, holding the port open and
# writing AT commands into it for the first several seconds after plug-in. The
# symptom is a driver that fails to initialize if started too quickly after
# connecting the base, then works on a retry -- which reads like a flaky cable.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", MODE:="0666", ENV{ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE}="1", SYMLINK+="ttySLATE"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# brltty
#
# The single most common reason a CH340 device does not appear on Ubuntu.
# brltty (braille display support, installed by default on Ubuntu desktop)
# ships /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/85-brltty.rules, which claims 1a86:7523 as a
# "BrlAPI" device and takes the port away moments after it enumerates. The
# symptom is exact and confusing: /dev/ttyUSB0 appears, then vanishes a second
# later, and `journalctl -u brltty` shows it binding.
#
# Trossen's own instructions say to remove the package outright:
#
#   sudo apt-get remove brltty
#
# setup-host.sh does that (with a prompt) rather than trying to out-rule it --
# rule precedence against a vendor-shipped file in /usr/lib is fragile, and
# nothing on this rig uses a braille display.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
